[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergences found and fixed while checking these rows
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m All were x86 NaN-payload / operand-order bugs, found by comparing `to_bits()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rather than float values, and diagnosed from `objdump -d` of the C `.so` (CMake
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m builds it at `-O0`, so GCC emits one SSE instruction per source operator and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *first* source operand — the one whose NaN payload survives — is always the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operator's left-hand side).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. `c2Dot` / `lm_dot2` — GCC emits the second product as `mulss(b.y, a.y)` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    makes it the *first* source of the `addss`. The Rust had
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `addss(mulss(a.x,b.x), mulss(a.y,b.y))`, which returned the wrong NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    payload. Fixed to `addss(mulss(b.y, a.y), mulss(a.x, b.x))`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. `c2CircletoCircle` — `A.r + B.r` is emitted as `addss(B.r, A.r)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. `f9` — five of the eight products in `invDenom`/`u`/`v` had swapped operands.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. `f11` — four of the `x + m` / `c + m` stores in the later hue sectors had
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    swapped operands (the original comments claimed "GCC swaps"; it does not).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. The `addss`/`subss`/`mulss`/`divss` helpers were written as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `if a.is_nan() {...} else if b.is_nan() {...} else { a op b }`. That is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    correct at `-O0` but **LLVM restructures it at `-O`**: `f9` returned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0xffc00000` instead of C's `0xffc58ed7` under `--release` while passing in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    debug. Rewritten as inline `asm!` so the instruction and operand order are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    opaque to the optimiser. Every row is now run in both profiles.
